<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palermo</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Panormus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palermo is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, on a bay of the island's north-western coast. Founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> and known to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> as Panormus, it was taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> in 831 and became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of Sicily and then of the Norman kingdom, whose first king was crowned there in 1130. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a> revolt began there in 1282.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palermo.jpg" alt="Palermo" width="320" height="349" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tango7174 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sicilia_Palermo5_tango7174.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Palermo lies in a basin formed by the Papireto, Kemonia and Oreto rivers, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> named the Conca d'Oro, the Golden Basin, in the 9th century. A mountain range forms a cirque around the city and faces the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrrhenian-sea">Tyrrhenian Sea</a>, and historically it made the interior of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> hard to reach. Monte Pellegrino, which stands apart in front of the sea, is the historically most important of the mountains, and Goethe called its cliff the most beautiful promontory in the world. Cave drawings at nearby Addaura show settlement perhaps around 8000 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The original inhabitants were the Sicani, who according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thucydides">Thucydides</a> arrived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>. In the 8th century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> built a settlement on the natural harbour, one of their three main colonies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> along with Motya and Soluntum, and the city later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>. As Panormus it was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a> and Empire for over a thousand years. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vandals">Vandals</a> under Geiseric took it in 440 CE, and in late 535 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belisarius">Belisarius</a> laid siege to the city, which had refused to surrender, and eventually prevailed. From 831 to 1072 Palermo was under Arab rule and for the first time the capital of Sicily.</p><p>After the Norman conquest it became the capital of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, which lasted from 1130 to 1816. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> ruled the city from 1479 until 1713, with brief interruptions, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> from 1720 to 1734. Charles III chose Palermo for his coronation as King of Sicily, but the royal court resided in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. In 1866 Palermo was the seat of a week-long popular rebellion against annexation by the Savoy kingdom, which was crushed under martial law, and the Teatro Massimo opened in 1897.</p><p>Allied bombing in 1942 and 1943 destroyed the harbour and much of the city, with heavy civilian casualties, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allied-invasion-of-sicily">Allied invasion of Sicily</a> captured it on 22 July 1943. In 1946 Palermo became the seat of the Regional Parliament, and in 1948 the element technetium was discovered at its university.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Palermo is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Its inhabitants speak Italian and the Palermitano dialect of Sicilian. The city is the main seat of the World Heritage Site Arab-Norman Palermo and the Cathedral Churches of Cefalù and Monreale, and its markets of Vucciria, Ballarò and Capo are known for fruit, vegetables and fish. Its patron saint is Saint Rosalia, whose feast day is 15 July.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palermo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Palermo</b> fell in 831 and became the base of Muslim rule; Messina followed in 842 or 843, Syracuse in 878 and Taormina in 902, though pockets of Christian resistance held out until 967.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Ibn Hawqal, a Baghdad merchant who visited <b>Palermo</b> in 950, reckoned 7,000 butchers trading in 150 shops in the city.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-sicily">Emirate of Sicily · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles shifted the centre of government from <b>Palermo</b> to Naples, where he built the Castel Nuovo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the eleventh century Norman adventurers led by the Hauteville family took Apulia and Calabria from the Byzantines and Lombard princes, and between 1061 and 1091 Count Roger I conquered Sicily from its Muslim emirs, taking <b>Palermo</b> in 1072 together with his brother Robert Guiscard.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sicily">Kingdom of Sicily · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Naples had over 400,000 inhabitants, the third largest city in Europe; <b>Palermo</b> was Italy's third.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-two-sicilies">Kingdom of the Two Sicilies · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Further west they settled at Utica and Carthage in North Africa, at Motya, <b>Panormus</b> and Soloeis in western Sicily, at Nora, Sulcis and Tharros in Sardinia, on Malta, and along the coasts of southern Spain and Morocco, where Gadir, modern Cádiz, beyond the Straits of Gibraltar, grew rich…</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li></ul></section>
